{
  "image_name": "ca7d88c8f89fde0fb161ec8b97dda47d.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes three biological routes for dye remediation. On the left, enzyme-assisted remediation shows azo dye breakdown by key oxidoreductive enzymes, especially azoreductase, laccase, and peroxidase. The central idea is cleavage or oxidation of the azo bond and related aromatic structures, using cofactors such as NADH/NAD+ or oxidants like oxygen and hydrogen peroxide, ultimately converting the dye into smaller products.\n\nThe middle section expands this to whole microorganisms, including bacteria, fungi, yeast, and algae. Dyes first seem to interact with extracellular polymeric substances and cell surfaces through biosorption, then undergo extracellular enzymatic degradation and intracellular mineralization. The visible end products are mainly CO2 and H2O, along with intermediates, indicating progressive detoxification rather than simple adsorption alone.\n\nThe right section shows phytoremediation by a plant. It combines several plant-based mechanisms: phytotransformation and rhizodegradation break down dyes with plant or root-associated enzymes; rhizofiltration captures dyes at the roots; phytoextraction moves and accumulates dyes in shoots; phytostabilization retains them near roots; and phytovolatilization releases transformed products as gases through transpiration. Overall, the figure compares enzymes, microbes, and plants as complementary systems for removing and transforming dye pollutants.",
